From: CosmosEx Buildroot <4372835+planeturban@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:24:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ce_update.sh: flash the chips when the app asks, also without an
 update

The image's ce_update.sh stops at once when there's no USB update ("no
online update"). But the app also runs it when it wants the chips
flashed: on a Xilinx HW/FW mismatch (the ACSI/SCSI jumper changed) it
writes /tmp/FW_FLASH_XILINX and the update script, then exits. The
script stopped, cesuper.sh started the app again, the app found the same
mismatch and exited: every 5 seconds, for good. Recovery level 3 (first
firmware, then a restart) didn't work either.

With FW_FLASH_XILINX, FW_FLASH_ALL or REBOOT_AFTER_UPDATE and no USB
update: stop the app, run check_and_flash_chips.sh, restart if asked.
---
--- a/ce_update.sh
+++ b/ce_update.sh
@@ -9,6 +9,24 @@
 # files in /ce, never the system or the image's own app. The image is
 # updated by writing a new image to the SD card (later: the image's own
 # update). An update from a USB stick (/tmp/UPDATE_FROM_USB) still works.
+#
+# The app also runs this script when it wants the chips flashed without an
+# update: a Xilinx HW/FW mismatch (the ACSI/SCSI jumper changed: it writes
+# /tmp/FW_FLASH_XILINX, then exits) and recovery level 3 (first firmware,
+# then a restart: /tmp/REBOOT_AFTER_UPDATE). Do that part here; stopping
+# early would bring the app back with the same mismatch every 5 seconds.
+if [ ! -f /tmp/UPDATE_FROM_USB ] && { [ -f /tmp/FW_FLASH_XILINX ] || [ -f /tmp/FW_FLASH_ALL ] || [ -f /tmp/REBOOT_AFTER_UPDATE ]; }; then
+    echo "Flashing the chips as the app asked (no update)."
+    /ce/ce_stop.sh $1 $2
+    /ce/update/check_and_flash_chips.sh
+    sync
+    if [ -f /tmp/REBOOT_AFTER_UPDATE ]; then
+        rm -f /tmp/REBOOT_AFTER_UPDATE
+        reboot now
+    fi
+    echo "ce_update.sh finished at: $( date )"
+    exit 0
+fi
 if [ ! -f /tmp/UPDATE_FROM_USB ]; then
     echo "Online update is not available on this image."
     echo "To update, write a new image to the SD card."
-- 
